🚀 Kubernetes HPA & VPA

Today I learned about Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) and Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) in Kubernetes and how they help applications automatically adjust to changing workloads.

Autoscaling is an important Kubernetes feature that helps maintain application performance, availability, and efficient resource utilization without requiring manual intervention.

🔹 Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)

Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) automatically adjusts the number of Pods running for a Kubernetes workload based on resource utilization or other supported metrics.

In simple terms:

HPA scales the number of Pods horizontally.

For example:

Low Traffic
    ↓
2 Pods

High Traffic
    ↓
5 Pods

Very High Traffic
    ↓
10 Pods

When the workload decreases, HPA can reduce the number of Pods again.

How HPA Works
Application
     ↓
    Pods
     ↓
Metrics Server
     ↓
     HPA
     ↓
Checks Metrics
     ↓
Adjusts Replicas
     ↓
Deployment
     ↓
More / Fewer Pods

HPA can use metrics such as:

CPU utilization
Memory utilization
Custom metrics
External metrics
Example HPA Configuration
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler

metadata:
  name: my-app-hpa

spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: my-app

  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 10

  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 50

Here:

minReplicas: 2 → Minimum 2 Pods.
maxReplicas: 10 → Maximum 10 Pods.
averageUtilization: 50 → HPA attempts to maintain average CPU utilization around 50%.
Important HPA Commands
kubectl get hpa
kubectl describe hpa <hpa-name>
kubectl get hpa -w
kubectl top pods
kubectl top nodes
🔹 Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA)

Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) automatically adjusts the CPU and memory resource requests/allocations of Pods based on their actual resource usage and recommendations.

In simple terms:

VPA makes individual Pods bigger or smaller in terms of CPU and memory resources.

For example:

Before VPA:

CPU:    250m
Memory: 256Mi

        ↓ VPA Recommendation

After:

CPU:    750m
Memory: 768Mi

Unlike HPA, VPA generally does not increase the number of Pods. Instead, it focuses on right-sizing the resources assigned to Pods.

🔹 How VPA Works
Application
     ↓
    Pods
     ↓
Resource Usage
     ↓
VPA Recommender
     ↓
CPU / Memory Recommendation
     ↓
VPA Controller
     ↓
Updates Pod Resources

VPA observes resource usage over time and generates recommendations for CPU and memory.

Example VPA Configuration
apiVersion: autoscaling.k8s.io/v1
kind: VerticalPodAutoscaler

metadata:
  name: my-app-vpa

spec:
  targetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: my-app

  updatePolicy:
    updateMode: "Auto"

  resourcePolicy:
    containerPolicies:
      - containerName: "*"

        minAllowed:
          cpu: "100m"
          memory: "128Mi"

        maxAllowed:
          cpu: "1"
          memory: "1Gi"
VPA Update Modes
Off
updateMode: "Off"

VPA only provides recommendations and doesn't automatically change Pods.

Initial
updateMode: "Initial"

Recommended resources are applied when Pods are initially created.

Recreate
updateMode: "Recreate"

VPA can recreate Pods when resource recommendations need to be applied.

Auto
updateMode: "Auto"

Allows VPA to automatically apply resource recommendations. Depending on the required change, Pods may be evicted/recreated.

🔥 HPA vs VPA
Feature	HPA	VPA
Full Name	Horizontal Pod Autoscaler	Vertical Pod Autoscaler
Scaling Type	Horizontal	Vertical
Changes	Number of Pods	CPU & Memory Resources
Main Purpose	Scale application capacity	Right-size resources
Example	3 Pods → 8 Pods	500m CPU → 1 CPU
Pod Count	Increases/Decreases	Usually unchanged
Resource Size	Doesn't primarily change it	Changes it
Best For	Variable traffic	Uncertain resource requirements
🧠 Easy Way to Remember
HPA → More / Fewer Pods
VPA → Bigger / Smaller Pods

Or:

HPA = Scale OUT / IN
VPA = Scale UP / DOWN
🔹 HPA Example in Real World

Imagine an e-commerce application normally running with:

3 Pods
CPU Usage = 30%

During a sale:

CPU Usage = 85%

HPA detects the increased workload and can scale:

3 Pods
   ↓
5 Pods
   ↓
8 Pods

When the sale ends and traffic decreases:

8 Pods
   ↓
5 Pods
   ↓
3 Pods

This allows the application to automatically adapt to traffic.

🔹 VPA Example in Real World

Suppose a background worker has:

CPU Request:    250m
Memory Request: 256Mi

But over time, the application consistently requires more resources.

VPA may recommend:

CPU Request:    750m
Memory Request: 768Mi

Instead of adding more Pods, VPA right-sizes the resources of the existing workload.

⚠️ HPA and VPA Together

HPA and VPA can be used together in some architectures, but they must be configured carefully.

A common concern is using HPA and VPA on the same CPU/memory utilization signal. VPA can change resource requests, which can affect the utilization calculation that HPA uses.

Therefore, combining them requires careful planning and testing.

A common conceptual design is:

                 Application
                     │
                     ↓
                  Pods
                /       \
               ↓         ↓
             HPA        VPA
              ↓          ↓
        Pod Count     CPU/Memory
         Scaling      Right-sizing
🎯 When to Use HPA?

Use HPA when:

Application traffic changes frequently.
Request volume increases/decreases.
You need more Pods during peak traffic.
You are running stateless applications.
You want automatic scale-out and scale-in.

Examples:

Web Applications
REST APIs
Microservices
E-commerce Applications
🎯 When to Use VPA?

Use VPA when:

CPU and memory requirements are difficult to predict.
Pods are consistently under-provisioned.
Pods are consistently over-provisioned.
You want to optimize resource requests.
You want automatic resource right-sizing.

Examples:

Background Workers
Batch Processing
Long-running workloads
Applications with unpredictable resource requirements
